\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Gaussian marginalization commutes with sector decomposition}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{H=X\mathbin\perp Y} be a finite-dimensional real
orthogonal sum, #{P=P_X\oplus P_Y} an orthogonal involution,
and #{Q} a positive self-adjoint precision commuting with #{P}.
Use induced Euclidean volumes and Borel Gaussian laws. For
#{Q(x,y)=(Ax+By,B^\dagger x+Cy)}, put
#{S=A-BC^{-1}B^\dagger}. The sign sectors are
#{H_\varepsilon=X_\varepsilon\mathbin\perp Y_\varepsilon}.
Let #{\pi(x,y)=x} and let #{\pi_\varepsilon} be its sector
restriction. The [typed restriction identity](fdip-003Q) gives
##{S_\varepsilon:=S|_{X_\varepsilon}
 =A_\varepsilon-B_\varepsilon C_\varepsilon^{-1}
 B_\varepsilon^\dagger.}
Then the following square commutes both for maps and for their
pushforwards of the Gaussian laws:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  map/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (h) at (0,0) {$H$};
\node (hs) at (4.5,0) {$H_+\times H_-$};
\node (x) at (0,-2.3) {$X$};
\node (xs) at (4.5,-2.3) {$X_+\times X_-$};
\draw[map] (h) -- node[above=2mm] {$D_H$} (hs);
\draw[map] (x) -- node[below=2mm] {$D_X$} (xs);
\draw[map] (h) -- node[left=2mm] {$\pi$} (x);
\draw[map] (hs) -- node[right=2mm] {$\pi_+\times\pi_-$} (xs);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Here #{D_H(z)=(E_+z,E_-z)} and #{D_X(x)=(F_+x,F_-x)}
are the orthogonal sector decompositions. The full retained law is
#{\pi_*\mu_Q=\mu_S}; both routes give
#{\mu_{S_+}\otimes\mu_{S_-}} on #{X_+\times X_-}.}

\proof{
\p{Testing positivity on #{(0,y)} makes #{C} positive and invertible.
Its inverse is self-adjoint by adjointing both inverse equations, so
#{S} is self-adjoint. With #{K=C^{-1}B^\dagger}, expansion gives
##{\langle(x,y),Q(x,y)\rangle
 =\langle x,Sx\rangle+\langle y+Kx,C(y+Kx)\rangle.}
The two cross terms sum to #{2\langle x,By\rangle}; the last
#{K}-term cancels the subtraction in #{S}. Testing on #{(x,-Kx)}
proves #{S>0}. Thus the hypotheses of
[positive Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D) hold. For every fixed #{x},
translation by #{Kx} preserves induced volume, giving the pointwise
fiber identity
##{\int_Y e^{-\langle(x,y),Q(x,y)\rangle/2}\,d\lambda_Y(y)
 =Z_C e^{-\langle x,Sx\rangle/2}.}
The normalizer is #{Z_C=(2\pi)^{\dim Y/2}/\sqrt{\det C}}.
Nonnegative Tonelli gives #{Z_Q=Z_CZ_S}, and inserting a Borel
indicator of #{x} then dividing proves #{\pi_*\mu_Q=\mu_S}.
This is the [retained marginal theorem](fdip-000E), with its actual
volume and positive finite normalizers.}

\p{The restricted #{Q_\varepsilon,C_\varepsilon,S_\varepsilon}
are positive self-adjoint. Applying the same fiber identity within
#{H_\varepsilon} gives, for every #{x_\varepsilon},
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{Y_\varepsilon}
 e^{-\langle(x_\varepsilon,y),Q_\varepsilon(x_\varepsilon,y)\rangle/2}
 \,d\lambda_{Y_\varepsilon}(y)\\
&\qquad=Z_{C_\varepsilon}
 e^{-\langle x_\varepsilon,S_\varepsilon x_\varepsilon\rangle/2}.
\end{aligned}}
Hence #{(\pi_\varepsilon)_*\mu_{Q_\varepsilon}=\mu_{S_\varepsilon}}.
The [orthogonal product theorem](fdip-000M), applied to #{Q,C,S},
gives their product laws and #{Z_C=Z_{C_+}Z_{C_-}}. No scalar
factor is discarded before normalization.}

\p{Since #{\pi P=P_X\pi}, the projector formulas imply
#{\pi E_\varepsilon=F_\varepsilon\pi}, proving the map square.
Pushforwards compose by inverse images. The upper route first gives
#{\mu_{Q_+}\otimes\mu_{Q_-}}. Its pushforward by
#{\pi_+\times\pi_-} agrees with
#{\mu_{S_+}\otimes\mu_{S_-}} on every Borel rectangle, using
the sector marginal identities, and therefore everywhere by uniqueness
of probability measures on the rectangle-generated Borel sigma-algebra.
The lower route first gives #{\mu_S}; its sector decomposition gives
the same product.}
}

\p{Pullback on the [polynomial function algebras](fdip-002V)
reverses all four arrows. For every polynomial #{f} on
#{X_+\times X_-}, the commuting map square gives
##{f\circ D_X\circ\pi
 =f\circ(\pi_+\times\pi_-)\circ D_H.}
Precomposition by each linear map is a unital algebra homomorphism,
as in [polynomial transport](fdip-003F). For a polynomial
#{p\in\operatorname{Pol}(X)}, nonnegative integration first gives
#{\int|p\circ\pi|\,d\mu_Q=\int|p|\,d\mu_S};
[absolute Gaussian moments](fdip-002W) make both sides finite for
polynomials. Subtraction of positive and negative parts then gives
#{\omega_S=\omega_Q\circ\pi^*} and the corresponding equalities
on every side of the square. This is equality of normalized
polynomial functionals, in addition to maps and probability laws.}

\p{The [specified conditional kernel](fdip-003A) at eliminated
field zero instead has precision #{A};
it need not equal the marginal of precision #{S}. If #{B=0}, then
#{S=A}, so equality can occur. The
[coupled cycle](fdip-003S) gives a concrete difference.
A [nonstable retained set](fdip-003L) does not supply this square:
for retention #{\{0,1\}}, #{\pi\delta_2=0} but
#{\pi P\delta_2=\delta_0}. A singular eliminated direction can
have infinite fiber integral, as for #{Q(x,y)=(x,0)} on
#{\mathbb R^2}. A pseudoinverse does not make that integral finite.
The [massless cycle](fdip-0038) has a constant zero mode and requires
a separately specified constrained law.}

\p{The Schur convention is
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}, with the
pivot exchanged to #{C}. The Gaussian density convention is
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}, where
covariance is #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}. The sector square is the direct
combination proved above; the formalization references discussed with
the [retained marginal theorem](fdip-000E) support
Gaussian images and signed Fubini with their stated hypotheses, not
the pointwise fiber constants.}
